ter;\n            vertical-align: middle;\n        }\n        .stats-col:not(:last-child) {\n            border-right: 1px solid #e2ecef;\n        }\n        .stats-number {\n            font-size: 24pt;\n            font-weight: bold;\n            color: #e74c3c;\n            margin-bottom: 5px;\n        }\n        .stats-label {\n            font-size: 9.5pt;\n            color: #546e7a;\n            font-weight: 600;\n        }\n\n        /* Blockquotes */\n        blockquote {\n            margin: 25px 0;\n            padding: 15px 20px;\n            background-color: #f9f9f9;\n            border-left: 4px solid #95a5a6;\n            font-style: italic;\n            color: #555;\n        }\n\n        /* Callout Box (Solution Section) */\n        .solution-box {\n            background-color: #f0f7f4;\n            border: 1px solid #d6e9df;\n            border-radius: 6px;\n            padding: 20px;\n            margin: 25px 0;\n        }\n        .solution-title {\n            font-weight: bold;\n            color: #27ae60;\n            margin-bottom: 10px;\n            font-size: 11.5pt;\n        }\n        .solution-list {\n            margin: 0;\n            padding-left: 20px;\n        }\n        .solution-list li {\n            margin-bottom: 10px;\n            text-align: justify;\n        }\n        .solution-list strong {\n            color: #2c3e50;\n        }\n\n        /* Case Study Section */\n        .case-study {\n            background-color: #fff9f0;\n            border: 1px solid #f3e5d0;\n            border-radius: 6px;\n            padding: 20px;\n            margin: 25px 0;\n            page-break-inside: avoid;\n        }\n        .case-title {\n            font-weight: bold;\n            color: #d35400;\n            margin-bottom: 10px;\n            font-size: 11.5pt;\n        }\n\n        /* Footer Call to Action */\n        .article-footer {\n            margin-top: 40px;\n            padding-top: 20px;\n            border-top: 2px dashed #e2ecef;\n            text-align: center;\n            background-color: #fafbfc;\n            padding: 25px;\n            border-radius: 6px;\n        }\n        .article-footer p {\n            text-align: center;\n            font-weight: bold;\n            color: #34495e;\n        }\n        .cta-button {\n            display: inline-block;\n            background-color: #1995ad;\n            color: white;\n            padding: 10px 24px;\n            border-radius: 4px;\n            text-decoration: none;\n            font-weight: bold;\n            margin-top: 10px;\n            font-size: 10.5pt;\n        }\n\n        /* Prevent orphan headings and single lines */\n        h1, h2, h3 {\n            page-break-after: avoid;\n        }\n        \n        /* Unordered lists default spacing */\n        .content-body ul:not(.solution-list) {\n            margin-bottom: 20px;\n            padding-left: 25px;\n        }\n        .content-body ul:not(.solution-list) li {\n            margin-bottom: 10px;\n            text-align: justify;\n        }\n    </style></head><body><div class="article-container"><!-- Website Header Brand --><div class="web-header">\n            GangSehat<span>.com</span></div><div class="content-body"><!-- Title --><h1>More Than Just a Broken Bone: 5 Surprising Facts About Wrist Injuries That Can Happen to Anyone</h1><!-- Metadata --><div class="meta-info"><span class="category">Health Info</span><span>• By: Physiotherapy Expert Team</span><span>• Website: gangsehat.com</span></div><!-- Intro --><p><strong>CRACK!</strong> The sound was sharp, followed immediately by a stinging pain that instantly paralyzed you. Imagine you were just walking casually, and suddenly you slipped on an oil spill. Instinctively, your hands reached out to break the fall. This "heroic" reflex is actually a natural self-preservation mechanism to prevent your head or chest from hitting the ground.</p><p>However, ironically, this rescue action often backfires, causing structural damage to the bones in your wrist—medically known as a <strong>distal radius fracture</strong>.</p><p>As the key to our daily productivity, from typing on a keyboard to driving, the wrist is an asset we often ignore until its function is compromised. Let's dissect five profound facts that will change how you view the health of this small yet vital joint.</p><!-- Fakta 1 & Statistics Section --><h2>1. Numbers Debunk the Myth: The Youth Are Actually Most Vulnerable</h2><p>Many people assume broken bones are an "elderly disease" that only haunts those with osteoporosis. However, medical data points to a contradictory and surprising reality. Cases of distal radius fractures are mostly found in the productive age group.</p><div class="stats-grid"><div class="stats-col"><div class="stats-number">21.7%</div><div class="stats-label">Ages 20-29 Years</div></div><div class="stats-col"><div class="stats-number">21.4%</div><div class="stats-label">Ages 10-19 Years</div></div><div class="stats-col"><div class="stats-number">64.1%</div><div class="stats-label">Male Patients</div></div></div><p>Why is that? High mobility and active lifestyles at a young age are the main risk factors, especially traffic accidents (as riders), which account for nearly half of all total cases.</p><blockquote>\n                "Based on gender characteristics, 434 (64.1%) male patients experienced distal radius fractures compared to 243 female patients." (Tantri, 2019).\n            </blockquote><!-- Fakta 2 --><h2>2. The "Reflex Mechanism" That Backfires</h2><p>This fracture usually occurs due to indirect trauma through an extreme backward-bending position of the hand (<em>hyperextension</em>). When you fall, the arm takes the axial load of your entire body weight with the elbow slightly bent.</p><p>Here lies the irony: the body's natural reflex to protect vital organs ends up striking the end of the radius bone with a force that exceeds its elasticity limit. This phenomenon proves how fragile our anatomy can be when faced with sudden external pressure. The hand that tries to save you becomes the "victim" of your own body weight.</p><!-- Fakta 3 --><h2>3. The "Dinner Fork" Deformity and Different Types of Fractures</h2><p>In the medical world, visual identification is a crucial first step because it determines the course of treatment. There are two classic types of fractures most commonly encountered:</p><ul><li><strong>Colles Fracture:</strong> This is the most common type. It happens when you fall on an outstretched, open palm, causing the bone fragments to displace posteriorly (backward). Visually, this creates a bump on the back of the wrist resembling a "dinner fork deformity".</li><li><strong>Smith Fracture:</strong> The exact opposite of a Colles fracture, this occurs from falling on the back of the hand. Here, the bone fragments displace anteriorly (forward).</li></ul><blockquote>\n                "The distal structure of the fracture (wrist and hand) displacing posteriorly causes what is known as a 'dinner fork deformity'."\n            </blockquote><!-- Fakta 4 & Case Study Section --><h2>4. When "The Doctor Becomes the Patient" – Psychological and Functional Impacts</h2><p>This injury does not discriminate by profession, and its impact can be highly debilitating for those who rely on manual precision. Let's look at a real-world evaluation:</p><div class="case-study"><div class="case-title">Clinical Case Study: Mrs. F (Doctor, 33 Years Old)</div><p>Mrs. F from Depok had a single-vehicle accident in July 2025. The hand that usually holds a stethoscope and surgical tools with extreme precision suddenly lost its basic abilities.</p><p>Through the <em>Wrist Hand Disability Index (WHDI)</em> evaluation, Mrs. F scored <strong>31 out of 50, or about 62%</strong>. On the clinical scale, this falls into the category of <em>severe disability</em> (high dependency).</p><p>Mrs. F experienced severe difficulties in performing simple tasks we often take for granted: writing, driving, even basic self-care like bathing. For a medical professional, losing this functional independence is not just a physical issue, but also a heavy psychological burden.</p></div><!-- Fakta 5 & Solution Box --><h2>5. The Secret to Recovery at Soerojo Hospital: Not Just Resting, But Measured Movement</h2><p>A common mistake post-surgery is assuming the hand must be "completely immobilized" to heal quickly. At Soerojo Hospital Magelang, physiotherapy protocols prove that the key to recovery actually lies in disciplined and measured mobilization.</p><div class="solution-box"><div class="solution-title">Integrated Physiotherapy Treatment Plan:</div><ul class="solution-list"><li><strong>Mobilization with Movement (MWM):</strong> This manual therapy technique is proven to increase the range of motion faster than standard exercises.</li><li><strong>Microwave Diathermy:</strong> Applying thermal effects for 15 minutes can increase tissue metabolism and reduce chronic pain.</li><li><strong>Ultrasound Therapy:</strong> Utilizing sound waves at the right dosage to relieve pain and accelerate regeneration in the fractured area.</li><li><strong>Strengthening Exercises (Stretching & Strengthening):</strong> Active and passive movements applied gradually are crucial to combat joint stiffness and restore muscle strength weakened during the recovery period.</li></ul></div><!-- Conclusion --><h2>6. Conclusion</h2><p>A distal radius fracture is a reminder of how quickly our productivity can be halted by just one minor incident. However, the good news is that a broken bone is not the end of the world. Through proper diagnosis and conservative yet progressive physiotherapy management, hand mobility functions can return to normal.</p><p>The return of functional independence, as seen in Mrs. F's case after routinely undergoing therapy, is living proof that there is always hope.</p><!-- Footer Call to Action --><div class="article-footer"><p>If our hands are the windows to our productivity, have we given them the proper attention and protection they deserve today?</p><a href="https://wa.me/6281346111417" class="cta-button">Consult with Our Physiotherapist</a></div></div></div></body></html>	Gang Sehat Team	Kesehatan, Tips, Nyeri Leher, Fisioterapi	https://gezenrryirjwnqyhuafx.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/service-images/blog/1779504451149-n0smgzlwohs.png	2026-05-23 02:47:37.263+00	2026-05-23 02:47:37.484345+00	2026-05-24 03:30:40.398+00	t				
